we take triangle \\(PQR\\) and cut off three smaller triangles at its vertices, we get the hexagon.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- At vertex \\(P\\), the cut is along segment \\(BC\\) (parallel to \\(QR\\)).\n- At vertex \\(Q\\), the cut is along segment \\(DE\\) (parallel to \\(RP\\)).\n- At vertex \\(R\\), the cut is along segment \\(FA\\) (parallel to \\(PQ\\)).\n\nEach cut removes an equilateral triangle? Wait — not necessarily equilateral, but the cut segments are parallel to the opposite side of the big triangle.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3 – Using similarity\nLet’s label the big triangle \\(PQR\\) with:\n\n\\[\nPQ = 200, \\quad QR = 240, \\quad RP = 300\n\\]\n\nThe hexagon sides are all length \\(s\\).  \nThe cut-off triangles at each vertex are similar to the big triangle.\n\nAt vertex \\(P\\), the cut-off triangle has base \\(BC = s\\) parallel to \\(QR\\).  \nThe ratio of similarity for that small triangle is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{s}{QR} = \\frac{s}{240}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- At \\(Q\\), cut-off triangle has base \\(DE = s\\) parallel to \\(RP = 300\\), ratio \\(\\frac{s}{300}\\).\n- At \\(R\\), cut-off triangle has base \\(FA = s\\) parallel to \\(PQ = 200\\), ratio \\(\\frac{s}{200}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Step 4 – Side length of big triangle in terms of \\(s\\)\nThe side \\(PQ\\) of the big triangle is made of three parts:\n\n- From \\(P\\) to the point where \\(FA\\) meets \\(PQ\\): this is part of the small triangle at \\(R\\).\n- The segment \\(FA\\) itself (length \\(s\\)).\n- From the point where \\(BC\\) meets \\(PQ\\) to \\(Q\\): part of small triangle at \\(P\\).\n\nLet’s be more precise:\n\nOn side \\(PQ\\) (length 200):\n\n- The small triangle at \\(R\\) has side along \\(PQ\\) of length \\(\\frac{s}{200} \\cdot 200 = s\\)? Wait, careful:  \nThe small triangle at \\(R\\) is similar to big triangle with ratio \\(\\frac{s}{200}\\) (since its base \\(FA\\) is parallel to \\(PQ\\) and length \\(s\\)).  \nSo the side of that small triangle along \\(PQ\\) has length \\(\\frac{s}{200} \\cdot 200 = s\\).  \nSimilarly, the small triangle at \\(P\\) has side along \\(PQ\\) of length \\(\\frac{s}{240} \\cdot 200 = \\frac{200s}{240} = \\frac{5s}{6}\\).\n\nThus:\n\n\\[\nPQ = s + \\frac{5s}{6} + \\text{(middle part?)} \n\\]\n\nWait — actually the middle part is the side \\(BC\\) of the hexagon? No, \\(BC\\) is not on \\(PQ\\). Let’s re-check.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5 – Correct decomposition\nDraw triangle \\(PQR\\).  \nThe hexagon is inside, with vertices:\n\n- \\(A\\) on \\(PR\\),  \n- \\(B\\) on \\(PQ\\),  \n- \\(C\\) on \\(PQ\\),  \n- \\(D\\) on \\(QR\\),  \n- \\(E\\) on \\(QR\\),  \n- \\(F\\) on \\(RP\\).\n\nActually, from the parallel conditions:\n\n- \\(AB\\) is part of line \\(PQ\\)? No — \\(AB\\) is a side of hexagon, not on the big triangle’s side.  \nBetter: The sides of the hexagon are inside, parallel to the big triangle’s sides.\n\nLet’s set coordinates for clarity.\n\n---\n\n### Step 6 – Coordinate approach\nPlace triangle \\(PQR\\) with:\n\n\\(P = (0,0)\\),  \n\\(Q = (200,0)\\),  \n\\(R\\) such that \\(PR = 300\\), \\(QR = 240\\).\n\nFind \\(R\\):  \nFrom \\(P\\): \\(R = (x,y)\\) with \\(x^2 + y^2 = 300^2\\).  \nFrom \\(Q\\): \\((x-200)^2 + y^2 = 240^2\\).\n\nSubtract:  \n\\(x^2 - (x-200)^2 = 300^2 - 240^2\\)  \n\\(x^2 - (x^2 - 400x + 40000) = 90000 - 57600\\)  \n\\(400x - 40000 = 32400\\)  \n\\(400x = 72400\\)  \n\\(x = 181\\)\n\nThen \\(y^2 = 300^2 - 181^2 = 90000 - 32761 = 57239\\), so \\(y = \\sqrt{57239}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Step 7 – Hexagon vertices\nSince \\(AB \\parallel DE\\), etc., the hexagon is formed by cutting off three similar triangles.\n\nLet the side length be \\(s\\).\n\nAt vertex \\(P\\), the cut-off triangle has sides along \\(PQ\\) and \\(PR\\) proportional to \\(s\\) relative to side \\(QR = 240\\).  \nSo the length along \\(PQ\\) from \\(P\\) to \\(B\\) is \\(\\frac{s}{240} \\cdot 200 = \\frac{5s}{6}\\).  \nSimilarly, along \\(PR\\) from \\(P\\) to \\(A\\) is \\(\\frac{s}{240} \\cdot 300 = \\frac{5s}{4}\\).\n\nAt vertex \\(Q\\), cut-off triangle has side along \\(QP\\) from \\(Q\\) to \\(C\\) of length \\(\\frac{s}{300} \\cdot 200 = \\frac{2s}{3}\\).  \nAlong \\(QR\\) from \\(Q\\) to \\(D\\): \\(\\frac{s}{300} \\cdot 240 = \\frac{4s}{5}\\).\n\nAt vertex \\(R\\), cut-off triangle has side along \\(RQ\\) from \\(R\\) to \\(E\\): \\(\\frac{s}{200} \\cdot 240 = \\frac{6s}{5}\\).  \nAlong \\(RP\\) from \\(R\\) to \\(F\\): \\(\\frac{s}{200} \\cdot 300 = \\frac{3s}{2}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Step 8 – Matching sides\nNow \\(AB\\) is the segment connecting \\(A\\) on \\(PR\\) to \\(B\\) on \\(PQ\\).  \nWe require \\(AB = s\\).\n\nCoordinates:\n\n\\(B\\) is on \\(PQ\\): \\(B = \\left( \\frac{5s}{6}, 0 \\right)\\).  \n\\(A\\) is on \\(PR\\): \\(P=(0,0)\\), \\(R=(181, \\sqrt{57239})\\).  \nFraction from \\(P\\) to \\(R\\) is \\(\\frac{5s/4}{300} = \\frac{5s}{1200} = \\frac{s}{240}\\).  \nSo \\(A = \\left( \\frac{s}{240} \\cdot 181, \\frac{s}{240} \\cdot \\sqrt{57239} \\right)\\).\n\nDistance \\(AB\\):\n\n\\[\nAB^2 = \\left( \\frac{181s}{240} - \\frac{5s}{6} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{s\\sqrt{57239}}{240} \\right)^2\n\\]\n\nSimplify first term:  \n\\(\\frac{181}{240} - \\frac{5}{6} = \\frac{181}{240} - \\frac{200}{240} = -\\frac{19}{240}\\).  \nSo first term squared: \\(\\frac{361 s^2}{57600}\\).\n\nSecond term: \\(\\frac{57239 s^2}{57600}\\).\n\nSum: \\(\\frac{361 + 57239}{57600} s^2 = \\frac{57600}{57600} s^2 = s^2\\).\n\nSo indeed \\(AB = s\\), consistent.\n\n---\n\n### Step 9 – Now use another side, say \\(BC\\)\n\\(B = \\left( \\frac{5s}{6}, 0 \\right)\\).  \n\\(C\\) is on \\(PQ\\) as well: from \\(Q\\) toward \\(P\\), length \\(\\frac{2s}{3}\\).  \n\\(Q = (200,0)\\), so \\(C = \\left( 200 - \\frac{2s}{3}, 0 \\right)\\).\n\nThen \\(BC = \\left( 200 - \\frac{2s}{3} \\right) - \\frac{5s}{6} = 200 - \\frac{4s}{6} - \\frac{5s}{6} = 200 - \\frac{9s}{6} = 200 - \\frac{3s}{2}\\).\n\nWe require \\(BC = s\\):\n\n\\[\n200 - \\frac{3s}{2} = s\n\\]\n\\[\n200 = s + \\frac{3s}{2} = \\frac{5s}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\ns = \\frac{400}{5} = 80\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Step 10 – Conclusion\nThe side length of the hexagon is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{80}\n\\]'}, 'events': [], 'links': [], 'resource': {'attributes': {'service.name': 'agentops', 'agentops.project.id': 'dummy', 'imported_libraries': ['string', 'sympy', 'uuid', 'json', 'asyncio']}, 'schema_url': ''}}, {'name': 'openai.chat.completion', 'context': {'trace_id': '0x967a34a19f45d67c900108fb5fe08bf8', 'span_id': '0x92bbd5574f23fd3b', 'trace_state': '[]'}, 'kind': 'SpanKind.CLIENT', 'parent_id': '0x5dccca7cfa73cd16', 'start_time': '2026-08-25T08:30:11.728103Z', 'end_time': '2026-08-25T08:30:13.697061Z', 'status': {'status_code': 'OK'}, 'attributes': {'gen_ai.request.type': 'chat', 'gen_ai.system': 'OpenAI', 'gen_ai.request.model': 'deepseek-v4-flash', 'gen_ai.request.max_tokens': 4000, 'gen_ai.request.temperature': 0, 'gen_ai.request.top_p': 0.99, 'gen_ai.request.streaming': False, 'gen_ai.prompt.0.role': 'system', 'gen_ai.prompt.0.content': 'You are a helpful assistant.', 'gen_ai.prompt.1.role': 'user', 'gen_ai.prompt.1.content': '\nTask: Evaluate if the current memory is complete and accurate enough to answer the query, or if more tools are needed.\n\nContext:\n- **Query:** Let ABCDEF be a convex equilateral hexagon in which all pairs of opposite sides are parallel. The triangle whose sides are extensions of segments AB, CD, and EF has side lengths 200, 240, and 300. Find the side length of the hexagon.\n\nWhen ready, output the final answer enclosed in <answer> and </answer> tags. Do not generate any content after the </answer> tag. When ready, output the final answer enclosed in <answer> and </answer> tags. Do not generate any content after the </answer> tag.\n- **Available Tools:** [\'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\']\n- **Toolbox Metadata:** {\'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\': {\'tool_name\': \'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\', \'tool_description\': \'A generalized tool that takes query from the user, and answers the question step by step to the best of its ability. It can also accept an image.\', \'tool_version\': \'1.0.0\', \'input_types\': {\'query\': \'str - The query that includes query from the user to guide the agent to generate response.\'}, \'output_type\': \'str - The generated response to the original query\', \'demo_commands\': [{\'command\': \'execution = tool.execute(query="Summarize the following text in a few lines")\', \'description\': \'Generate a short summary given the query from the user.\'}], \'user_metadata\': {\'limitation\': \'\\nThe Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool may provide hallucinated or incorrect responses.\\n\', \'best_practice\': "\\nFor optimal results with the Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool:\\n1. Use it for general queries or tasks that don\'t require specialized knowledge or specific tools in the toolbox.\\n2. Provide clear, specific query.   \\n3. Use it to answer the original query through step by step reasoning for tasks without complex or multi-step reasoning.\\n4. For complex queries, break them down into subtasks and use the tool multiple times.\\n5. Use it as a starting point for complex tasks, then refine with specialized tools.\\n6. Verify important information from its responses.\\n"}, \'require_llm_engine\': True}}\n- **Initial Analysis:** <answer>\n**Summary of Query:**  \nThe problem involves a convex equilateral hexagon ABCDEF with opposite sides parallel. Extensions of AB, CD, and EF form a triangle with side lengths 200, 240, and 300. The goal is to find the side length of the hexagon.\n\n**Necessary Skills:**  \n1. **Geometry (Polygons & Parallel Lines):** Understanding properties of equilateral hexagons with opposite sides parallel (affine-regular hexagon) and how extensions of alternate sides form a triangle.  \n2. **Triangle Similarity & Proportions:** Relating the large triangle’s side lengths to the hexagon’s side length via similar triangles formed by the hexagon’s sides.  \n3. **Algebraic Manipulation:** Setting up and solving equations for the unknown side length.\n\n**Necessary Tools:**  \n- **Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool:** This tool can provide a step-by-step solution by reasoning through the geometry and algebra. It is suitable because the problem is well-defined and does not require specialized computational tools; the tool can derive the relationship between the triangle’s side lengths and the hexagon’s side length.\n\n**How the Tool Helps:**  \n- It can break down the problem into logical steps: identify the triangle formed by extensions, use parallel lines to establish similar triangles, express the triangle’s sides in terms of the hexagon’s side length, and solve for the unknown.  \n- It can handle the algebraic derivation and produce the final numeric answer.\n\n**Additional Considerations:**  \n- The tool may hallucinate; verify the reasoning.  \n- The problem likely has a unique solution; the side length is the same for all sides due to equilateral property.  \n- The triangle’s side lengths (200, 240, 300) are not arbitrary; they relate to the hexagon’s side length via a scaling factor.  \n- Ensure the convexity and parallelism conditions are correctly used to set up the similar triangles.\n\n**Final Answer (derived via tool):**  \nThe side length of the hexagon is **60**.\n</answer>\n- **Memory (Tools Used & Results):** {\'Action Step 1\': {\'tool_name\': \'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\', \'sub_goal\': \'Determine the side length of the hexagon using given triangle side lengths.\\n\\n4.\', \'command\': \'execution = tool.execute(query="Let ABCDEF be a convex equilateral hexagon in which all pairs of opposite sides are parallel. The triangle whose sides are extensions of segments AB, CD, and EF has side lengths 200, 240, and 300. Find the side length of the hexagon.")\', \'result\': [\'We have a convex equilateral hexagon \\\\(ABCDEF\\\\) with all opposite sides parallel.  \\nThat means:\\n\\n- \\\\(AB \\\\parallel DE\\\\)\\n- \\\\(BC \\\\parallel EF\\\\)\\n- \\\\(CD \\\\parallel FA\\\\)\\n\\nAlso, all sides are equal: \\\\(AB = BC = CD = DE = EF = FA = s\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 1 – Understanding the geometry\\nIf opposite sides are parallel, the hexagon is centrally symmetric.  \\nIn fact, a convex equilateral hexagon with opposite sides parallel is a *zonogon* (centrally symmetric polygon).  \\nFor such a hexagon, the three pairs of opposite sides form three directions.\\n\\nIf we extend sides \\\\(AB\\\\), \\\\(CD\\\\), and \\\\(EF\\\\), they form a triangle.  \\nThe sides of that triangle are given as 200, 240, 300.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 2 – Relating hexagon side to triangle sides\\nLet’s label the triangle formed by extending \\\\(AB\\\\), \\\\(CD\\\\), and \\\\(EF\\\\) as \\\\(\\\\triangle XYZ\\\\):\\n\\n- \\\\(AB\\\\) extended meets \\\\(CD\\\\) extended and \\\\(EF\\\\) extended to form one vertex.\\n- Similarly for the other two.\\n\\nBecause the hexagon is equilateral, each side of the hexagon is parallel to one side of this outer triangle, and the hexagon is inscribed in the triangle in a certain way.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 3 – Known property\\nFor a centrally symmetric hexagon with equal sides, the side length \\\\(s\\\\) is related to the triangle formed by three alternate sides by:\\n\\n\\\\[\\ns = \\\\frac{2 \\\\cdot \\\\text{Area of triangle}}{\\\\text{Perimeter of triangle}}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWait, let’s check:  \\nActually, there is a known result: If you extend three alternate sides of such a hexagon, the triangle formed has side lengths equal to sums of certain segments.  \\nBut here, the hexagon is equilateral, so each side of the triangle is made up of two hexagon sides plus a gap.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 4 – Diagram reasoning\\nLet’s place the hexagon so that:\\n\\n- \\\\(AB\\\\) is horizontal.\\n- \\\\(BC\\\\) goes up-right.\\n- \\\\(CD\\\\) goes up-left.\\n- \\\\(DE\\\\) horizontal left.\\n- \\\\(EF\\\\) down-left.\\n- \\\\(FA\\\\) down-right.\\n\\nExtend \\\\(AB\\\\) to the right, \\\\(CD\\\\) upward, \\\\(EF\\\\) downward-left.  \\nThey meet to form a large triangle.\\n\\nThe side of the triangle along the direction of \\\\(AB\\\\) will be:  \\nFrom the intersection of \\\\(AB\\\\) and \\\\(EF\\\\) to the intersection of \\\\(AB\\\\) and \\\\(CD\\\\).  \\nThat length equals \\\\(s + s +\\\\) some extra? Let’s compute carefully.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 5 – Vector approach\\nLet the side vectors be:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbf{u} = \\\\overrightarrow{AB}, \\\\quad \\\\mathbf{v} = \\\\overrightarrow{BC}, \\\\quad \\\\mathbf{w} = \\\\overrightarrow{CD}\\n\\\\]\\nThen:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\overrightarrow{DE} = -\\\\mathbf{u}, \\\\quad \\\\overrightarrow{EF} = -\\\\mathbf{v}, \\\\quad \\\\overrightarrow{FA} = -\\\\mathbf{w}\\n\\\\]\\nAll have length \\\\(s\\\\).\\n\\nThe hexagon is closed:  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbf{u} + \\\\mathbf{v} + \\\\mathbf{w} - \\\\mathbf{u} - \\\\mathbf{v} - \\\\mathbf{w} = 0\\n\\\\]\\nautomatically.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 6 – Triangle from extensions\\nLine \\\\(AB\\\\) is along \\\\(\\\\mathbf{u}\\\\).  \\nLine \\\\(CD\\\\) is along \\\\(\\\\mathbf{w}\\\\).  \\nLine \\\\(EF\\\\) is along \\\\(-\\\\mathbf{v}\\\\).\\n\\nThe triangle vertices are intersections of:\\n\\n- \\\\(AB\\\\) and \\\\(CD\\\\): call \\\\(P\\\\)\\n- \\\\(CD\\\\) and \\\\(EF\\\\): call \\\\(Q\\\\)\\n- \\\\(EF\\\\) and \\\\(AB\\\\): call \\\\(R\\\\)\\n\\nThe side \\\\(PQ\\\\) lies along \\\\(CD\\\\) and \\\\(EF\\\\) intersection? Wait, careful:  \\nActually \\\\(P\\\\) = intersection of \\\\(AB\\\\) and \\\\(CD\\\\),  \\n\\\\(Q\\\\) = intersection of \\\\(CD\\\\) and \\\\(EF\\\\),  \\n\\\\(R\\\\) = intersection of \\\\(EF\\\\) and \\\\(AB\\\\).\\n\\nSo side \\\\(PQ\\\\) is along \\\\(CD\\\\) direction, length = distance between intersections along that line.  \\nSimilarly for others.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 7 – Lengths in terms of \\\\(s\\\\)\\nBecause the hexagon is equilateral, the distance between the intersection points along each direction is exactly \\\\(2s\\\\) times some factor? Let’s check with vectors.\\n\\nTake \\\\(AB\\\\) direction:  \\nPoint \\\\(A\\\\) to \\\\(B\\\\) is \\\\(\\\\mathbf{u}\\\\).  \\nExtend \\\\(AB\\\\) beyond \\\\(B\\\\) to meet \\\\(CD\\\\) extended.  \\nSimilarly extend \\\\(AB\\\\) backward beyond \\\\(A\\\\) to meet \\\\(EF\\\\) extended.\\n\\nThe distance between these two intersection points along \\\\(AB\\\\) direction equals \\\\(s + s +\\\\) the projection of the other sides?  \\nActually, the triangle side along \\\\(AB\\\\) direction equals the sum of the lengths of the projections of the other two side vectors onto that direction, plus \\\\(2s\\\\)? Let’s do systematically.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 8 – Coordinate setup\\nLet \\\\(\\\\mathbf{u} = (s,0)\\\\).  \\nLet \\\\(\\\\mathbf{v} = (s\\\\cos 120^\\\\circ, s\\\\sin 120^\\\\circ) = \\\\left(-\\\\frac{s}{2}, \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s\\\\right)\\\\).  \\nLet \\\\(\\\\mathbf{w} = \\\\left(-\\\\frac{s}{2}, -\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s\\\\right)\\\\).  \\nThis makes the hexagon symmetric.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 9 – Find intersections\\nLine \\\\(AB\\\\): through \\\\(A=(0,0)\\\\) direction \\\\((1,0)\\\\).  \\nLine \\\\(CD\\\\): starts at \\\\(C = A+\\\\mathbf{u}+\\\\mathbf{v} = \\\\left(s - \\\\frac{s}{2}, \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s\\\\right) = \\\\left(\\\\frac{s}{2}, \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s\\\\right)\\\\), direction \\\\(\\\\mathbf{w} = \\\\left(-\\\\frac12, -\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\right)\\\\) scaled by \\\\(s\\\\).\\n\\nLine \\\\(EF\\\\): starts at \\\\(E = A+\\\\mathbf{u}+\\\\mathbf{v}+\\\\mathbf{w} = (0,0) + (s,0) + \\\\left(-\\\\frac{s}{2}, \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s\\\\right) + \\\\left(-\\\\frac{s}{2}, -\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s\\\\right) = (0,0)\\\\). Wait, that’s back to A? That can’t be right — oh, that means \\\\(E\\\\) coincides with \\\\(A\\\\)? That would mean the hexagon is degenerate. So my choice of angles is wrong.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 10 – Correct angle choice\\nFor a convex equilateral hexagon with opposite sides parallel, the three directions are separated by 60° each.  \\nLet’s take:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbf{u} = (s,0), \\\\quad \\\\mathbf{v} = \\\\left(\\\\frac{s}{2}, \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s\\\\right), \\\\quad \\\\mathbf{w} = \\\\left(-\\\\frac{s}{2}, \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s\\\\right)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n- \\\\(A = (0,0)\\\\)\\n- \\\\(B = (s,0)\\\\)\\n- \\\\(C = \\\\left(\\\\frac{3s}{2}, \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s\\\\right)\\\\)\\n- \\\\(D = \\\\left(s, \\\\sqrt{3}s\\\\right)\\\\)\\n- \\\\(E = (0, \\\\sqrt{3}s)\\\\)\\n- \\\\(F = \\\\left(-\\\\frac{s}{2}, \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s\\\\right)\\\\)\\n\\nCheck: \\\\(AB\\\\) horizontal, \\\\(BC\\\\) up-right, \\\\(CD\\\\) up-left, \\\\(DE\\\\) left, \\\\(EF\\\\) down-left, \\\\(FA\\\\) down-right. Opposite sides parallel: yes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 11 – Triangle from extensions\\nLine \\\\(AB\\\\): \\\\(y=0\\\\).  \\nLine \\\\(CD\\\\): through \\\\(C\\\\) and \\\\(D\\\\), slope = \\\\(\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}s - \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s}{s - \\\\frac{3s}{2}} = \\\\frac{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s}{-\\\\frac{s}{2}} = -\\\\sqrt{3}\\\\).  \\nEquation: \\\\(y - \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s = -\\\\sqrt{3}(x - \\\\frac{3s}{2})\\\\).\\n\\nLine \\\\(EF\\\\): through \\\\(E\\\\) and \\\\(F\\\\), slope = \\\\(\\\\frac{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s - \\\\sqrt{3}s}{-\\\\frac{s}{2} - 0} = \\\\frac{-\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s}{-\\\\frac{s}{2}} = \\\\sqrt{3}\\\\).  \\nEquation: \\\\(y - \\\\sqrt{3}s = \\\\sqrt{3}(x - 0)\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 12 – Intersections\\n- \\\\(AB\\\\) and \\\\(CD\\\\): set \\\\(y=0\\\\) in CD equation:  \\n\\\\(0 - \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s = -\\\\sqrt{3}(x - \\\\frac{3s}{2})\\\\)  \\n\\\\(\\\\Rightarrow -\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s = -\\\\sqrt{3}x + \\\\frac{3\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s\\\\)  \\n\\\\(\\\\Rightarrow -\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s - \\\\frac{3\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}s = -\\\\sqrt{3}x\\\\)  \\n\\\\(\\\\Rightarrow -2\\\\sqrt{3}s = -\\\\sqrt{3}x\\\\)  \\n\\\\(\\\\Rightarrow x = 2s\\\\).  \\nSo \\\\(P = (2s, 0)\\\\).\\n\\n- \\\\(C