ing all possible row/column color patterns and checking maximality).  \n- It outputs the final numeric answer, which can be verified.\n\n**Additional Considerations:**  \n- The tool may hallucinate; the answer should be cross-checked manually or via logical verification.  \n- The problem is non-trivial; the tool may need to be prompted to reason step-by-step and consider all edge cases (e.g., empty rows/columns, all chips same color, etc.).  \n- Since chips are indistinguishable, only the pattern of colors (white/black/empty) matters, not which specific chip is placed.  \n- The maximality condition implies that every empty cell must be in a row and column that already have chips of different colors (or one of them is empty), making it impossible to add a chip without violating uniformity.\n</answer>\n- **Memory (Tools Used & Results):** {\'Action Step 1\': {\'tool_name\': \'Generalist_Solution_Generator_Tool\', \'sub_goal\': \'- To generate possible configurations for placing white and black chips on a 5×5 grid satisfying all given constraints using an automated method.\\n\\n4.\', \'command\': \'execution = tool.execute(query="There is a collection of 25 indistinguishable white chips and 25 indistinguishable black chips. Find the number of ways to place some of these chips in the 25 unit cells of a 5x5 grid such that: each cell contains at most one chip, all chips in the same row and all chips in the same column have the same colour, and any additional chip placed on the grid would violate one or more of the previous two conditions. Please solve this step by step and provide the final answer.")\', \'result\': [\'We have a 5×5 grid, and we place chips such that:\\n\\n1. Each cell has at most one chip.\\n2. In each row, all chips are the same color.\\n3. In each column, all chips are the same color.\\n4. The arrangement is *maximal* — you cannot add another chip without breaking rule 2 or 3.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Step 1 – Understanding the constraints\\n\\nIf in a row the